rrived at their advice and more or less  landed it on the government on sunday  without warning there's an aspect of  of of this that i think is worth just  bringing to your attention for  a moment and that's that the head of the  government's body of public health  experts a guy called dr tony houlihan  who was very much the face of this  strong  medical and scientific advice to lock  down during the first phase  in the spring he's been off on  compassionate family leave  for the last number of months but on  sunday he returned actually a day  earlier than he was supposed to return  he arrived he was due back at his desk  on monday he arrived on sunday  called a meeting and issued the uh the  lockdown  recommendations and this was viewed in  government  by many people and a number of ministers  said it to me  as sort of a power play if you like a  signal that  look dr tony is back he's taking control  we have to go back to lockdown but the  government took um  as you say a very different view has the  government  made clear why it doesn't agree with the  scientific advice it's had  it had two problems i suppose with the  way  with with the actions of the public  health experts on sunday  the first was the manner in which they  did it a very quick meeting  to make the recommendations without  going through the sort of normal  channels that you might expect both  formally  and informally with uh with the  government they simply  landed the uh the recommendation to go  back to lockdown  on the government on sunday night not  entirely  uh without warning there had been some  back channeling but nothing uh to the  extent  that gave the government what they felt  was adequate warning so  governments were annoyed at at the  procedure the process that they followed  but they also took the view  on the substance of the public health  experts advice that while they didn't  dispute the grounds on which they were  making the advice it's very clear to  everybody that numbers have been going  up alarmingly  over the last couple of weeks their view  was that they had to make  a decision which took into other factors  and the minister for finance  for instance was very strong both in  public and in  private that to take the advice of the  public experts and go straight  to a lockdown would cost hundreds of  thousands of jobs  over the coming weeks over the coming  weeks jobs that would not come back  and in his view in the view of most of  his fellow ministers  uh they needed to they needed to see if  a less  strict lockdown over the coming weeks  would  would work so their view was that they  had to take into  uh they had to take into consideration  other elements not just  economic but other social elements as  well  as well as just the public health advice  in making their decision  wouldn't one of the things that  influenced the politicians be  though as you say the number of cases in  ireland is rising  still only about 490 on a daily  basis deaths are two  the seven day moving average of deaths  are two and seven days ago  the seven day moving energy of deaths  were two i mean  maybe a lot of people would think well  if that's the fatality rate  then the government was maybe right not  to follow  not to lock down the economy on the  basis of two  deaths with no sign yet that that death  rate is rising  and this is one of the questions i think  that has to be answered at the moment  you're right about the  the the death rate the figures for today  is there there's only 157 people  in hospital with this illness today  there's only 27 people across the whole  uh the whole of the republic in  intensive care units now of course the  uh worry from public uh public health  doctors  is that you know the the numbers  infected and the numbers that need to be  admitted to hospital and and and and  thereafter into icu  units uh increases very quickly you get  this exponential rise in case and  all of a sudden within a relatively  short period of time the public health  system  is is overwhelmed but there's no sense  of that happening at the moment so  while cases have certainly been  increasing  uh very significantly over the last two  two and a half weeks or so  the admissions to hospital and the  admissions to intensive care units of  hospitals  and the debts have not been rising and  in that sense  so far at least this second wave in  ireland at least i think we see this in  other countries as well  is much less serious by those measures  than the first wave was so the question  i suppose that policy makers and medics  will have to answer  over the coming weeks is whether this  second wave  is permanently and qualitatively  different to the first wave  are whether the hospital admissions the  icu admissions and the debts  will catch up over the coming weeks and  that was one of the things  i think that fed into the government's  decision early on this week  not to move to to another lockdown was  that they felt they had the latitude  over the coming weeks  to wait and see if those indicators  catch up with the number of cases  so pat where does this leave now uh the  scientists and the politicians i mean  you wrote  the relationship between the government  and the scientific advisors  has been fundamentally reset i saw the  irish examiner saying that mr varadkar  the deputy t shock didn't just throw dr  houlihan the chief medical officer under  the bus  he reversed the bus over him again and  again  so what quantum relations like that's a  reference to an interview that mr  veracle gave on television on monday  night in which  as the examiner said it he pretty much  did drive the bus over  uh over tony houlihan now the two men  have since  had a conversation and they say that  they are looking forward to working  together etc etc over uh  over the coming weeks but what has  changed  very clearly between the public health  experts on one side of the government on  the other is that  early on in this pandemic during the  first wave the then government  basically made a virtue of saying we are  not going to make the decisions here  the doctors are going to make the  decisions we will at all times  take the public health doctor's advice  and that's what gave ireland  it's pretty quick pretty draconian  fairly extensive and ultimately quite  successful locked down  in in terms of how it suppressed the  virus uh before the summer  but at this stage in this second wave i  think what the government is saying  given the economic damage that has been  done  and the sort of pressure that the social  fabric both collectively and  individually has been  under in many cases they've taken the  view that  while deaths and icu admissions remain  under control  they have got to take a whole heap of  other factors  into consideration when they're making  their decisions and like it or not that  means that the  public health advice is only the  scientific advice  is only one factor that they consider  rather than the only factor that they  consider so in that respect  the relationship has changed profoundly  i think all right final question on this  before we  move on but i hope you stay with us  because i'm sure people have questions  to ask  because the irish experience is  fascinating to to follow  i assume that the prime minister the t  shock uh has got complete  coalition ireland has always been  governed  but is being governed by a coalition  that he's  he's got the coalition around him  in support of this because if it turned  out to be wrong  and the government actually had to move  to  tougher lockdown steps that would  probably be a political crisis for the  government  yeah i attended the press conference  late in government buildings on monday  night when this uh  after this decision was enhanced and the  health minister  you know when asked this question said  look you know it's not about who's right  and who's wrong here  well you know i think it might actually  turn out 